{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPkaRiBfauf9AiViVFwD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I hit a wall that matters for this piece: the WebSearch permission wasn't granted, so I couldn't verify anything against actual sources. For a researched piece running under your byline tonight, I'm not going to hand you citations typed from memory dressed up as print-ready — that's how a correction happens.\n\nHere's what I can honestly give you: the substance, tiered by how confident I am, with the bibliographic details marked where they need a five-minute check.\n\n## Turnspit dog — fact sheet (draft, citations unverified)\n\n**Tier 1 — solid, safe to write from**\n\n- A turnspit dog was a working kitchen animal, not a pet breed: it ran inside a wooden wheel mounted on the wall near the hearth, geared by chain or pulley to a roasting spit, keeping meat turning evenly over an open fire.\n- The physical type was consistent and deliberate — long-bodied, short, often crooked legs, low centre of gravity. Bred for the job, not for looks.\n- Dogs commonly worked in pairs on alternating shifts, because the work was punishing and continuous during a roast.\n- The cause of extinction is mechanical and economic, not disease or persecution: cheap spit-jacks — smoke jacks driven by rising hot air up the chimney, and spring-driven clockwork jacks — displaced the dog through the late 18th and 19th centuries. The shift from open-hearth roasting to closed cast-iron ranges finished the job. Rising anti-cruelty sentiment ran alongside this but followed the technology rather than led it.\n- Because it was never a fancy or kennel-club breed, it had no constituency to preserve it. It disappeared without anyone deciding to let it go.\n- A taxidermied specimen known as \"Whiskey\" survives in a Welsh museum collection (Abergavenny) — the most commonly reproduced physical evidence of the type. **Verify the museum and the specimen's date before printing.**\n\n**Tier 2 — sources I'm confident exist, details you must check**\n\n- **John Caius**, *Of Englishe Dogges* (1576) — Abraham Fleming's English translation of Caius's Latin *De Canibus Britannicis* (1570). Contains what's generally treated as the earliest English description of the turnspit. *Check: Fleming's exact spelling of the term, and which edition you cite.*\n- **Thomas Bewick**, *A General History of Quadrupeds* (Newcastle, 1790) — has a \"Turnspit\" entry with the well-known woodcut. *Check: edition and page; Bewick went through many editions and the plates move.*\n- **Edward Jesse**, *Anecdotes of Dogs* (London, 1846) — 19th-century descriptive material on turnspits in use. *Check the publisher; I believe Richard Bentley but would not print it unchecked.*\n- **Linnaeus**, *Systema Naturae* — the binomial *Canis vertigus* is routinely attributed here. *Check which edition actually carries it; this is frequently miscited.*\n\n**Tier 3 — the thing you specifically asked for, and where I have to stop**\n\nYou asked when the last working turnspit dogs were recorded. I don't have a firm, citable date I'd stand behind. The commonly repeated range is that working dogs persisted in some British and American kitchens into the 1870s–1880s and the type was effectively gone by about 1900 — but \"commonly repeated\" is exactly the status of a claim that needs a primary source, and it's the sentence in your piece most likely to be quoted back at you. The related strand worth chasing is Henry Bergh and the ASPCA, credited with ending turnspit use in New York hotel kitchens in the 1860s–70s; I can't point you to the document.\n\n**Two claims to handle carefully**\n\n- The idea that \"every dog has his day\" comes from alternating turnspit shifts is folk etymology. The phrase is older. Don't let it into the piece as fact.\n- Ancestry links to the Glen of Imaal Terrier or Welsh Corgi are speculation, not established.\n\n**Fastest verification path